    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205594": {
      "title": "Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - AI Safety Foundations",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - AI Safety Foundations Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - AI Safety Foundations Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - AI Safety Foundations Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Computing, Watson College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Oct 7, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 1756 Position ID: 205594 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: The School of Computing in the Thomas J. Watson College of Engineering and Applied Science at Binghamton University (The State University of New York at Binghamton) invites applications for a senior faculty-level or equivalent position, with an expected start date of September 1, 2027. The primary focus of this search is on established academic researchers for appointment at the tenured Associate Professor or Full Professor level, as appropriate. Exceptionally accomplished senior research and technical leaders from industry, government, or research laboratories will also be considered for an appropriate appointment. We seek an established scholar and research leader with an outstanding record of externally sponsored research and scholarly impact in the broad area of AI  Safety Foundations. We are particularly interested in candidates who will advance the mathematical and theoretical foundations of AI safety and develop rigorous frameworks for evaluating, verifying, and, where possible, guaranteeing the safety of advanced AI systems. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Foundational Limits and Mathematical Theory : Physical limits of AI inference; information-theoretic limits of AI safety verification; and physics-informed machine learning for energy systems. Formal Methods and Verification : Formal verification of neural network properties; formal verification for non-attentional sequential models; certified robustness with provable adversarial bounds; and AI metrology. Physics-Informed and Physics-Constrained AI : Physics-informed principles for AI design, physics-constrained machine learning, and the development of AI systems that incorporate physical laws and domain constraints. AI Hardware and Energy-Efficient Computing : AI hardware architectures, energy-efficient machine learning, and the theoretical and technological foundations of safe, efficient, and reliable AI computing. The successful candidate will provide intellectual leadership in this rapidly evolving field, develop a nationally and internationally recognized research program, and pursue significant external funding from federal and state agencies, industry, and other sponsors. The candidate will be expected to lead and participate in large-scale, multidisciplinary research initiatives and strengthen collaborative research across the School of Computing, the Watson College, and other academic and research units at Binghamton University and the broader SUNY system. As a senior faculty hire, the successful candidate will contribute to expanding the School of Computing's research portfolio and strategic capabilities. The candidate will help build a vibrant research community through collaboration, mentorship, and the recruitment of outstanding faculty, postdoctoral researchers, and graduate students. The candidate will also contribute to undergraduate and graduate education and mentor students and junior faculty, as appropriate to the candidate's appointment and background. Research Environment The position will be affiliated with the newly launched $55 million New York Center for AI Responsibility and Research (CAIRR) at Binghamton University - one of the nation's emerging centers focused on safe, responsible, and trustworthy artificial intelligence. The Center brings together researchers working across the technological, societal, and policy dimensions of AI and is intended to serve as a premier AI research hub for the State University of New York system, advancing transparent, accountable, secure, and public-focused AI technologies. The successful candidate will have an opportunity to help shape the Center's research agenda and develop collaborative initiatives at the intersection of AI safety, cybersecurity, trustworthy computing, AI-enabled software engineering, and responsible AI. The position is particularly well suited to a scholar or senior research and technology leader interested in building interdisciplinary research teams and pursuing ambitious, externally funded research programs addressing emerging challenges. Candidates from industry, government, or research laboratories should demonstrate a record of substantial technical or research leadership and the ability to contribute to the Center's research mission through research collaboration, technology development, industry partnerships, sponsored research, translation of research into practice, and/or other forms of high-impact innovation. The School of Computing and the Watson College are committed to building a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative environment for teaching, research, and professional engagement. We particularly welcome candidates who have demonstrated a commitment to fostering diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging through their teaching, mentoring, research, and professional activities. Historically underrepresented minorities, women, persons with disabilities, and veterans are strongly encouraged to apply. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications Doctoral degree in Computer Science, Software Engineering, Computer Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated record of externally sponsored research commensurate with appointment at the Associate or Full Professor level. Record of peer-reviewed scholarly publications and demonstrated research impact in an area relevant to the position. Record of effective teaching and research mentoring at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels. For exceptional candidates whose primary experience is outside a traditional academic setting, equivalent evidence of research leadership, technical innovation, and significant impact may be considered in evaluating qualifications for the position. Preferred Qualifications Outstanding and sustained record of externally sponsored research, including significant support from federal agencies and/or industry. National or international recognition for scholarly contributions in theoretical and foundational AI research or a closely related area. Demonstrated success in leading or contributing to large-scale, interdisciplinary, multi-institutional, or industry-academic research collaborations. Demonstrated ability to develop and lead ambitious research initiatives and pursue major collaborative funding opportunities. Experience mentoring graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and/or junior faculty and building high-performing research teams. Evidence of translating research into practice through industry partnerships, software systems, technology development, technology transfer, standards development, policy engagement, or other forms of societal impact. Research expertise that complements and strengthens existing capabilities in AI, cybersecurity, trustworthy computing, autonomous and physical AI systems, quantum technologies, microelectronics, or other strategic areas at Binghamton University. Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205594 Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the vacancy is filled. After filling out contact information, you will be directed to an upload page where you must submit: a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests, a statement of teaching interests, a diversity statement, and names and contact information of three references. For candidates whose primary professional experience is in non-academic setting, the application materials should clearly describe their research and development leadership, sponsored research or externally supported technical programs, major technical accomplishments, and contributions to research or technology innovation. Deadline for Applications: Open until filled. To check/edit your profile or to upload additional documents, please log into http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/login.jsp. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205592": {
      "title": "Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - Secure AI and Agentic Systems",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - Secure AI and Agentic Systems Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - Secure AI and Agentic Systems Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Senior Faculty-Level or Equivalent Position - Secure AI and Agentic Systems Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Computing, Watson College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Oct 7, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 1471 Position ID: 205592 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: The School of Computing in the Thomas J. Watson College of Engineering and Applied Science at Binghamton University (The State University of New York at Binghamton) invites applications for a senior faculty-level or equivalent position, with an expected start date of September 1, 2027. The primary focus of this search is on established academic researchers for appointment at the tenured Associate Professor or Full Professor level, as appropriate. Exceptionally accomplished senior research and technical leaders from industry, government, or research laboratories will also be considered for an appropriate appointment. We seek an established scholar and research leader with an outstanding record of externally sponsored research and scholarly impact in the broad area of secure, trustworthy, and agentic artificial intelligence . We are particularly interested in candidates whose research addresses the security, privacy, and complex dynamics of AI systems, from underlying hardware and computational infrastructure through deployment and operation. The ideal candidate will develop rigorous approaches to securing the AI lifecycle?from silicon to deployment?and to understanding, managing, and mitigating the behavior of interacting AI systems. Research in this area may draw upon hardware, software, cryptographic, formal, and systems-level methods to establish verifiable trust to strengthen privacy guarantees and improve the security and reliability of AI systems. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Hardware Security, Privacy, and Trustworthy AI Infrastructure : Developing architectures, protocols, and verification mechanisms that make AI systems trustworthy and resilient against adversarial attacks. Relevant research may include privacy-preserving federated learning, model-weights chain of custody, silicon-rooted inference trust, forensic designs for hardware and systems, and side-channel attacks on GPU inference. Agentic and Complex AI Systems : Developing methods to model, detect, and prevent emergent failures, cascading risks, and undesirable behaviors when autonomous AI agents interact with one another and with complex environments. Relevant research may include inter-domain cascade modeling for coupled infrastructure, agentic market stability, critical infrastructure interdependency modeling, and AI safety benchmarking. The successful candidate will provide intellectual leadership in this rapidly evolving field, develop and sustain a nationally and internationally recognized research program, pursue significant external funding from federal agencies, industry, and other sponsors, and contribute to major interdisciplinary research initiatives. The candidate will also be expected to build collaborative research programs across the School of Computing, the Watson College, and other academic and research units across Binghamton University and the broader SUNY system. As a senior faculty hire, the successful candidate will contribute to expanding the School of Computing's research portfolio and strategic capabilities. The candidate will help build a vibrant research community through collaboration, mentorship, and the recruitment of outstanding faculty, postdoctoral researchers, and graduate students. The candidate will also contribute to undergraduate and graduate education and mentor students and junior faculty, as appropriate to the candidate's appointment and background. Research Environment The position will be affiliated with the newly launched $55 million New York Center for AI Responsibility and Research (CAIRR) at Binghamton University - one of the nation's emerging centers focused on safe, responsible, and trustworthy artificial intelligence. The Center brings together researchers working across the technological, societal, and policy dimensions of AI and is intended to serve as a premier AI research hub for the State University of New York system, advancing transparent, accountable, secure, and public-focused AI technologies. The successful candidate will have an opportunity to help shape the Center's research agenda and develop collaborative initiatives at the intersection of AI safety, cybersecurity, trustworthy computing, AI-enabled software engineering, and responsible AI . The position is particularly well suited to a scholar or senior research and technology leader interested in building interdisciplinary research teams and pursuing ambitious, externally funded research programs addressing emerging challenges. Candidates from industry, government, or research laboratories should demonstrate a record of substantial technical or research leadership and the ability to contribute to the Center's research mission through research collaboration, technology development, industry partnerships, sponsored research, translation of research into practice, and/or other forms of high-impact innovation. The School of Computing and the Watson College are committed to building a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative environment for teaching, research, and professional engagement. We particularly welcome candidates who have demonstrated a commitment to fostering diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging through their teaching, mentoring, research, and professional activities. Historically underrepresented minorities, women, persons with disabilities, and veterans are strongly encouraged to apply. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications (Required) Doctoral degree in Computer Science, Software Engineering, Computer Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated record of externally sponsored research commensurate with appointment at the Associate or Full Professor level. Record of peer-reviewed scholarly publications and demonstrated research impact in an area relevant to the position. Record of effective teaching and research mentoring at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels. For exceptional candidates whose primary experience is outside a traditional academic setting, equivalent evidence of research leadership, technical innovation, and significant impact may be considered in evaluating qualifications for the position. Preferred Qualifications Outstanding and sustained record of externally sponsored research, including significant support from federal agencies and/or industry. National or international recognition for scholarly contributions in secure AI, trustworthy computing, AI-enabled software engineering, agentic systems, or a closely related area. Demonstrated success in leading or contributing to large-scale, interdisciplinary, multi-institutional, or industry-academic research collaborations. A demonstrated ability to develop and lead ambitious research initiatives and pursue major collaborative funding opportunities. Experience mentoring graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and/or junior faculty and building high-performing research teams. Evidence of translating research into practice through industry partnerships, software systems, technology development, technology transfer, standards development, policy engagement, or other forms of societal impact. Research expertise that complements and strengthens existing capabilities in AI, cybersecurity, trustworthy computing, autonomous and physical AI systems, quantum technologies, microelectronics, or other strategic areas at Binghamton University. Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205592 Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the vacancy is filled. After filling out contact information, you will be directed to an upload page where you must submit: a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests, a statement of teaching interests, a diversity statement, and names and contact information of three references. For candidates whose primary professional experience is in non-academic setting, the application materials should clearly describe their research and development leadership, sponsored research or externally supported technical programs, major technical accomplishments, and contributions to research or technology innovation. Deadline for Applications: Open until filled. To check/edit your profile or to upload additional documents, please log into http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/login.jsp. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
