    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205353": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Spanish (Golden Age)",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Spanish (Golden Age) Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Spanish (Golden Age) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Spanish (Golden Age) Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Department of Romance Languages, Harpur College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 30, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 3446 Position ID: 205353 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: The Department of Romance Languages and Literatures at Binghamton University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Spanish specializing in Golden Age literature. We seek a scholar whose research and teaching focus on Spanish literature of the sixteenth and/or seventeenth centuries and who can contribute broadly to our undergraduate and graduate curriculum in Spanish literature and culture. The successful candidate will be prepared to teach courses in Golden Age literature, including works by major authors such as Miguel de Cervantes, Pedro Calderón de la Barca, Garcilaso de la Vega, Luis de Góngora, Lope de Vega, Tirso de Molina, and Francisco de Quevedo. Scholars whose research and teaching extend into Medieval Spanish literature or Transatlantic Studies are especially encouraged to apply. We particularly welcome candidates whose work engages literature through interdisciplinary approaches or across historical periods. The position calls for a scholar who will bring new perspectives to the study of Spanish literature and demonstrates innovative approaches to teaching that engage students and connect the study of earlier periods to contemporary intellectual and cultural questions. The teaching load for this position is two Spanish courses per semester. The Department of Romance Languages consists of 30 faculty members across Spanish, French, and Italian. Our department teaches more than 1,000 students each semester and offers a variety of extracurricular programs for additional exposure to language and culture outside the classroom. Students can major or minor in any of our languages. Our Spanish division also offers three distinct courses for heritage speakers of the language. Within Harpur College of Arts and Sciences, Spanish is the second most commonly declared minor among students. We offer a range of graduate options, including 4+1 and traditional Master of Arts degrees in each of our languages, as well as a Master of Arts in Teaching in Spanish and French. Our faculty regularly collaborate with other units on campus, including the Center for Medieval and Renaissance Studies, the Translation Research and Instruction Program, the Latin American and Caribbean Studies Program, and the Linguistics Program. Additional information about the Department is available at https://www.binghamton.edu/romance/ Binghamton University is dedicated to advancing equity and inclusion, and in particular to the goal of building a diverse and inclusive teaching, research, and working environment, including a diverse faculty. Evidence of a commitment to advancing equity and inclusion through research, teaching, and/or service will be valued. Potential applicants who share this goal, especially members of groups historically underrepresented in the field and those from non-traditional backgrounds, women, and persons with disabilities, are strongly encouraged to apply. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Spanish or a related discipline by August 16, 2027 An active research agenda within Spanish Golden Age literature, as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications or work in progress Native or near-native fluency in Spanish and English Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching Spanish undergraduate or graduate courses Candidates whose research or teaching extends into Medieval Spanish literature Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: To apply, submit the following via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205353 Curriculum vitae; Cover letter describing your area of expertise, experience, and interest in the  position; Research statement; Teaching statement; Teaching evaluations from two courses; each evaluation must be from a different semester Contact information for three references from whom we might request confidential letters of recommendation. Complete applications received by November 2, 2026 will receive full consideration. The position will remain open until filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205251": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Biomedical Engineering Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Biomedical Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor in Biomedical Engineering Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Department of Biomedical Engineering, Watson College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 5937 Position ID: 205251 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: Department Overview: BU is ranked one of the top 50 public universities and is a premier R1 research university. The University's direct impact on the regional economy has reached $1.2 billion. The recent $113 million research award from the Economic Development Administration (EDA) further catalyzes and fosters knowledge transfer and partnerships across industries and academia to advance technology development and innovation in science and engineering, and a $160 million NSF ENGINE grant helps the university to build ecosystems for science discovery and technology advancement. The university attracts outstanding students and award-winning faculty, including 2019 Nobel Laureate Dr. Stanley Whittingham, who are partners in active discovery. The Department has recently launched a workforce training program partnered with the School of Management to develop a Biomanufacturing Project Management Certificate Program funded by BioMADE. The Department also runs a National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) PREP postdoc and graduate training program. The Biomedical Engineering (BME) Department is particularly strong in advanced biomanufacturing, biomaterials, biomechanics, cardiovascular engineering, nanomedicine, neuroengineering, organ-on-a-chip, stem cells, tissue engineering/regenerative medicine, and wearable biosensors. The BME Department is the home of the Center of Biomanufacturing for Regenerative Medicine (CBRM), and the Department has recently formed a joint Institute for Regenerative Medicine with Upstate Medical University. For more information, please visit our website . Funding from private operational support and public capital investment has allowed Binghamton University to establish the New York Center for AI Responsibility and Research , one of the nation's first centers aimed at ethical AI research. The Center will serve as a premier AI research hub for the entire State University of New York system and focus on developing novel technical innovations related to the safety and security of artificial intelligence models and systems. Job Description: As part of the strategic plan for expanding biomedical and health science research in emerging fields, the Department of Biomedical Engineering in the Thomas J. Watson College of Engineering and Applied Science at Binghamton University invites applications for one tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level with an expected start date of September 1, 2027. We seek applications from candidates in all areas of biomedical engineering; however, we are particularly interested in the areas of biomedical robotics and embodied intelligence for health and medicine. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, artificial intelligence (AI)-enabled physiological monitoring, biomechatronics, digital twins, human-machine interfaces, intelligent medical devices and instrumentation, implantable and wearable sensors and devices, rehabilitation and assistive technologies, and surgical and image-guided robotics. Candidates with a demonstrated commitment to diversity and inclusiveness are preferred. Responsibilities of the position include: Expand existing institutional capabilities in biomedical robotics and embodied intelligence for health and medicine through innovative research and external funding. Drive research initiatives and foster interdisciplinary collaborations within Binghamton University's New York Center for AI Responsibility and Research. Develop new undergraduate and graduate courses, teach and mentor students, and contribute to departmental growth. The successful candidate will receive a comprehensive startup package including competitive salary compensation and benefits commensurate with qualifications and experience. Requirements: Required Qualifications: PhD degree in biomedical engineering or a closely related discipline Preferred Qualifications: Background in biomedical robotics Industry or national laboratory experience Teaching experience Demonstrated commitment to diversity and inclusiveness Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205251 Applicants should submit: cover letter curriculum vitae statement of research interest statement of teaching interest statement of your contribution to diversity names and contact information of three (3) references Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205227": {
      "title": "Professor of Empire Innovation, Regenerative Medicine",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Professor of Empire Innovation, Regenerative Medicine Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Professor of Empire Innovation, Regenerative Medicine Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Professor of Empire Innovation, Regenerative Medicine Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Department of Biomedical Engineering, Watson College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 25, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 5502 Position ID: 205227 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: Department Overview: A premier public R1 research institution, Binghamton University contributes over $1.2 billion annually to the regional economy. The recent $113 million research award from Economic Development Agency (EDA) for battery technology innovation speaks to the university's leadership on knowledge transfer and partnerships across industrial and academia to advance technology development and innovation in science and engineering. The university attracts outstanding students and is home to award-winning faculty, many of whom are ranked in the top 2% of researchers in their respective fields, including the 2019 Nobel Laureate Dr. Stanley Whittingham in the field of Chemistry. The Biomedical Engineering Department is particularly strong in advanced biomanufacturing, biomaterials, biomechanics, cardiovascular engineering, nanomedicine, neuroengineering, organ-on-a-chip, stem cells, tissue engineering/regenerative medicine, and wearable biosensors. The Department has recently launched a workforce training program partnered with the School of Management to develop a Biomanufacturing Project Management Certificate Program funded by BioMADE. The Department also runs a National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) PREP postdoc and graduate training program. For more information, please visit our website . Job Description: The Thomas J. Watson College of Engineering and Applied Science at Binghamton University invites applications for a tenured Full or Associate Professor position with an expected start date of September 1, 2027, in regenerative medicine. We are seeking national leaders in stem cell science, cell therapy, and biomanufacturing with special focus in pancreatic islet development, islet biomanufacturing, islet biology, and islet transplantation to be part of a collaborative research and education alliance between Binghamton University and Upstate Medical University. This position will carry the title of SUNY Empire Innovation Professor and is supported in part by the State University of New York (SUNY)'s Empire Innovation Program (EIP), a key initiative to recruit and retain outstanding faculty with an established track record of research and scholarship in areas of strategic priority. We are seeking exceptional and well-established experts with outstanding track records of funded research, scholarship, teaching and service. Applications are invited from candidates in all areas of regenerative medicine, biomaterials, and tissue biofabrication. However, human pancreatic islet development, islet biomanufacturing, islet biology and islet transplantation are areas of particular interest. Successful candidates will augment existing capabilities and expertise in regenerative medicine at Binghamton University and Upstate Medical University, build on ongoing research at both campuses and further strengthen and grow cross-institutional collaborations. They will develop and implement a robust plan to synergize and accelerate research partnerships between the Center of Biomanufacturing for Regenerative Medicine (CBRM) led by Watson College's Biomedical Engineering Department and the Basic Science and Clinical Departments at Upstate Medical University. Successful candidates will receive a comprehensive startup package including competitive salary compensation and benefits commensurate with qualifications and experience. Requirements: Required Qualifications: MD or PhD degree in medicine, science or engineering Proven research leadership Record of scholarship as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications and citations Successful record of securing external funding Experience as an effective educator and research mentor Preferred Qualifications: Background in tissue engineering and cell or molecular biology specializing in stem cell research Demonstrated commitment to diversity and inclusiveness Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205227 Applicants should submit: cover letter curriculum vitae statement of research interest statement of teaching interest statement of your contribution to diversity names and contact information of three (3) references Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
