    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205082": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Environmental Health",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Assistant or Associate Professor of Environmental Health Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant or Associate Professor of Environmental Health Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant or Associate Professor of Environmental Health Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Department of Environment and Sustainability, Harpur College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 1704 Position ID: 205082 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: Binghamton University invites applications for a tenure track Assistant or Associate Professor of Global Public Health in the area of Environmental Health beginning Fall 2027. Candidates who recognize and investigate disparities in environmental health risk and/or environmental justice as part of their scholarship and teaching, particularly in the global context, are encouraged to apply. Qualified candidates should be able to teach effectively the scientific basis of environmental health as well as applicable policy issues. Applicants should show a strong program of research.  Applicants at the associate level should demonstrate success in securing funding and capacity for program leadership. The selected candidate will be part of the core faculty in Binghamton University's new undergraduate Global Public Health program ( https://www.binghamton.edu/harpur/global-public-health/ ) with tenure berth in the Department of Environment & Sustainability ( https://www.binghamton.edu/environmental-studies/ ). Our new colleague will teach two courses per semester including core courses in the Global Public Health program as well as others in their area of expertise. The Global Public Health program provides transdisciplinary training for students broadly interested in health and particularly interested in disease prevention. We are especially interested in scholars who will capitalize on collaboration opportunities at the university. The Department of Environment & Sustainability is an interdisciplinary department focused on understanding and addressing the complex environmental and sustainability challenges facing communities, nations, and the planet. Through interdisciplinary education, community-engaged learning, and applied research, faculty members in the department examine the dynamic relationship between human societies and the natural world and prepares students to understand, communicate about, and develop solutions to environmental challenges. Diversity, equity, and inclusion are critical global public health concerns and are at the core of the Global Public Health program's and the Department of Environment & Sustainability's teaching and research goals. Binghamton University is committed to attracting and retaining a diverse faculty, staff, and student body, and to honoring the experiences, perspectives, and unique identities of all of our faculty and students. Candidates for this position should describe how they will actively contribute to our mission of advancing a culture of diversity, equity, and inclusion through their research, teaching, mentoring, and service, and through their engagement with fellow faculty, students, and staff. Requirements: Required qualifications: PhD by August 16, 2027 in Environmental Health or a related field, such as Public Health, Environmental Science, Geography, etc. Preferred qualifications: Master's in global public health or health education, or public health Experience securing external research funding Experience teaching at the university level Experience engaging students in research Experience with community-based collaborative research with diverse communities. Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: To apply, submit the following documents via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205082 Cover letter with particular focus on research activities, including track record of or potential for external funding support Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching philosophy statement Names and contact information for at least three references Applications submitted by November 9, 2026 will receive full consideration. Materials will be considered until the position is filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205079": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor in the School of Computing",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Associate or Full Professor in the School of Computing Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Associate or Full Professor in the School of Computing Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate or Full Professor in the School of Computing Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Computing, Watson College Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 13524 Position ID: 205079 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: Position Title: Associate or Full Professor Department/Division: School of Computing The School of Computing in the Thomas J. Watson College of Engineering and Applied Science at Binghamton University (The State University of New York at Binghamton) invites applications for a tenure-track positions at the Associate and Full Professor level with an expected start date of January 1, 2027. We are looking for excellent candidates in the area of AI and Society. We invite candidates in areas including, but not limited to: AI Safety, Trustworthiness, and Adversarial Systems : Research on adversarial manipulation of AI systems, bot detection, coordinated inauthentic behavior, platform integrity, and mechanisms for building robust and trustworthy AI systems at scale. Advanced Computing Methods and Hardware for Scalable, Safe, and Responsible AI : Research on advanced computing methods enabling energy efficient high performance computing, quantum computing, agentic computing, edge computing, and provably safe computing hardware and software for scalable, safe, and responsible AI systems. Software Engineering with AI : Research on software engineering methods with AI tools including but not limited to large language models, AI agents, and complex agentic systems. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related discipline by the appointment date. Strong evidence of research accomplishments and commitment to teaching are essential. The School of Computing and the Watson College are dedicated to the goal of building a diverse and inclusive teaching, research, and working environment. We are particularly interested in candidates with a commitment to diversity and inclusiveness. Historically underrepresented minorities, women, persons with disabilities, and veterans, are strongly encouraged to apply. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications (Required) Ph.D. in computer science or a relevant field by December 31, 2026 Record of research College-level teaching experience Preferred Qualifications (Optional) The following qualifications are preferred but not required: Academic research credentials in publications and sponsored research Educational or teaching experience in a U.S.-based setting Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: To apply, submit the following documents via Interview Exchange portal/link:  http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205079 Cover letter (at the beginning of your cover letter please provide a single sentence indicating your primary teaching and primary research interest areas) Curriculum Vitae (CV) Teaching statement Research statement Diversity statement (which describes your experience and evidence of teaching a diverse population of students, including first generation students, and students from diverse racial and ethnic backgrounds, and international students, and how these experiences would contribute to enhancing the diversity and climate of the school, college, university) Names and contact information for three (3) references Evaluation of application materials begins immediately and continues until the positions are filled. For more information, visit https://www.binghamton.edu/computer-science/ Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205076": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Management Information Systems",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Management Information Systems Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Management Information Systems Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Management Information Systems Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: School of Management Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 1440 Position ID: 205076 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: School Overview: The School of Management (SOM) holds separate AACSB accreditations in Business and Accounting and is nationally recognized for its success in developing future business leaders.  SOM is listed as one of the top business schools among fully public institutions by Poets&Quants.  The School offers bachelor's, master's, and Ph.D. programs.  SOM consistently ranks among the top business schools for our undergraduate and graduate business programs. For more information about the School of Management, visit our website: http://www.binghamton.edu/som/. Job Description: The School of Management (SOM) at Binghamton University (State University of New York at Binghamton) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level in Management Information Systems, with an emphasis on Operations and Supply Chain Management, starting mid-August 2027, subject to budgetary approval. The successful candidate will demonstrate potential for excellence in both research and teaching.  A key requirement of the position is that the candidate should have appropriate academic training and preparation to teach Operations, Supply Chain Management, and related courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels in a business school environment. The position is housed within the Management Information Systems area. We are especially interested in candidates whose scholarship develops meaningful connections among information systems and operational or organizational processes. Relevant research may examine, for example, technology-enabled operations, artificial intelligence and automation, supply chains, service systems, process innovation, or how emerging technologies transform operational capabilities and organizational processes. We welcome candidates from information systems, operations management, or closely related fields whose research complements the expertise of our existing faculty and contributes to the intellectual community of the MIS area. The anticipated start date for this position is August 16, 2027. Position Responsibilities: Produce original and innovative research suitable for publication in leading academic journals. Teach Operations Management and related courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels.  The standard teaching load is 2+2 per academic year. Participate actively in the MIS Ph.D. program, including doctoral student mentoring and research development. Participate in the development and delivery of the MIS online program and other relevant academic programs. Contribute to the School, University, and professional community through committee service, shared governance, academic review activities, and other forms of professional engagement. The salary and benefits package are competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications.  To support faculty research, the School of Management provides summer funding for the first three years of service. Requirements: Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. in business administration with a specialization in management information systems, operations management, or a closely related field, -OR- completion of all degree requirements by the academic year obligation start date (August 16, 2027). Demonstrated potential to establish a successful program of scholarly research. Evidence may include published articles, manuscripts under review, working papers, conference presentations, or other research intended for publication in distinguished academic journals. Preferred Qualifications: Research that bridges information systems and operations and supply chain management. Experience teaching core Operations Management, technology and operations management, supply chain management, process improvement, or related courses. Experience in teaching or developing online courses. Please be advised that H1B visa sponsorship may not be available for this position. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205076 Cover letter or letter of motivation; Curriculum Vitae (CV); Research statement describing the candidate's research program and its principal contributions; One research writing sample, such as a published article, manuscript under review, or working paper; Teaching statement describing the candidate's teaching philosophy and experience.  The statement should also specifically address the approach to teaching Operations Management in a business school environment; Teaching evaluations, if available; A Ph.D. status statement for ABD candidates indicating dissertation status and the anticipated schedule for degree completion; and Contact information for three references.  Reference letters will be requested by the Search Committee Chair at a subsequent stage in the recruitment process. You may add additional files/documents after uploading your resume. After you fill out your contact information, you will be directed to the upload page. Please login to check/edit your profile or to upload additional documents: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/login.jsp. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled.  Preliminary interviews with shortlisted candidates will be conducted via Zoom. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205042": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor in Social Work",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Assistant or Associate Professor in Social Work Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant or Associate Professor in Social Work Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant or Associate Professor in Social Work Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Department of Social Work, CCPA Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 22, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 1279 Position ID: 205042 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: Department Overview: The Department of Social Work is entering an exciting period of growth and innovation. In addition to expanding its Bachelor of Social Work (BSW) and Master of Social Work (MSW) programs, the Department is actively developing a PhD program in Social Work, creating new opportunities for faculty leadership in doctoral education, research mentorship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and the advancement of the social work profession. The development of the PhD program reflects both the Department's growth and Binghamton University's broader commitment to expanding high-impact graduate and doctoral education within its R1 research environment. The Department is part of College of Community and Public Affairs (CCPA), an interdisciplinary college focused on preparing students for careers and leadership in public and community service. CCPA brings together Social Work, Human Development, Public Administration and Policy, Higher Education and Student Affairs, and Teaching, Learning and Educational Leadership, alongside the interdisciplinary Community Research and Action PhD program. The College emphasizes social justice, interdisciplinary collaboration, applied scholarship, and meaningful engagement with local, regional, national, and global communities. Located in downtown Binghamton, CCPA is closely connected to a broad network of community agencies, schools, health and human service organizations, government entities, and nonprofit partners. The Department offers a growing BSW program, a long-standing in-person MSW program, and an exclusively online MSW program, including full-time, part-time, and advanced standing pathways. The Department's programs are grounded in social justice, multisystem practice, community engagement, and a commitment to preparing social workers to address complex social problems across individuals, families, organizations, and communities. Both the Department and CCPA value applied, community-engaged scholarship that generates knowledge while contributing to meaningful improvements in the lives of individuals, families, organizations, and communities. Faculty collaborate extensively with colleagues across the University as well as with regional agencies, health and human service organizations, schools, nonprofit organizations, and government partners. Faculty members contribute to the intellectual and professional life of the Department through teaching, research, advising and mentoring, service, community engagement, and participation in program development and accreditation activities. Job Description: Binghamton University is seeking faculty members at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor to join its Department of Social Work within the College of Community and Public Affairs (CCPA). We seek scholar-educators whose teaching, research, and community engagement contribute to the Department's mission and strengthen our growing academic portfolio. We value inclusive, student-centered, competency-based pedagogy and seek faculty who can engage students across multiple learning modalities. We are particularly interested in scholars whose work advances evidence-informed and ethically grounded social work practice and addresses pressing challenges in areas such as: behavioral health child and family well-being child welfare health and mental health substance use aging social policy community development and other areas of contemporary social work practice and research Key Responsibilities: Teach approximately two courses per semester across the Department's BSW, in-person MSW, and exclusively online MSW programs. Contribute to the continued development of the Department's BSW, MSW, and emerging doctoral programs, as well as to interdisciplinary initiatives across CCPA and Binghamton University. Mentor students in research and scholarship as the doctoral program develops Establish and sustain an active program of scholarship with a strong potential for external funding, such as: federal agencies such as NIH, HRSA, ACF, and DoD foundations other external sponsors Binghamton University offers a collegial, collaborative environment within a nationally recognized R1 research institution. Faculty benefit from competitive salaries, research support and start-up resources, and extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and community-engaged scholarship. Binghamton's research enterprise is supported by substantial institutional investment and a university-wide commitment to translating research into meaningful impact. Diversity and Inclusion: Binghamton University remains committed to and supports diversity and inclusion as integral to academic excellence. We actively seek applications from underrepresented racial/ethnic groups, sexual minorities, veterans, persons with disabilities, and women. Requirements: Requirements: Doctoral degree conferred by August 1, 2027 Experience teaching online asynchronous, and/or in person (traditional) environments Scholarly research and teaching that incorporates inclusive pedagogical approaches Commitment to creating high-quality learning environments that respect diverse perspectives and comply with federal non-discrimination laws Demonstrated ability to conduct publishable research and pursue external funding Preferred: PhD in social work or social welfare MSW degree from a CSWE-Accredited Institution At least two years post-MSW, full-time social work experience Familiarity with CSWE accreditation standards and evolving federal compliance requirements (e.g., Title IX) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. If you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet eligibility requirements. Additionally, please note that Binghamton University is not an E-Verify employer. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205042 Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until 1/15/2027. Please submit: 1. Resume, 2. Cover letter, and 3. Contact information for three professional references You may add additional files/documents after uploading your resume. After you fill out your contact information, you will be directed to the upload page. Please login to check/edit your profile or to upload additional documents: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/login.jsp. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
    },
    "https://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205023": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor in Statistics, Social Work",
      "description": "Binghamton University\nJob Posting: Open Rank Professor in Statistics, Social Work Binghamton University \n\tJob Posting: Open Rank Professor in Statistics, Social Work Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Open Rank Professor in Statistics, Social Work Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Department of Social Work, CCPA Locations: Binghamton, NY Posted: Sep 21, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 3262 Position ID: 205023 Share About Binghamton University: Binghamton University is a premier public R1 research institution in the State University of New York (SUNY) system that unites more than 130 broadly interdisciplinary educational programs with some of the most vibrant research in the nation. Our unique character - shaped by our mission, outstanding academics, facilities, and community life - promotes extraordinary student success, research, and service; Binghamton University is where our students, faculty and staff thrive. Working at Binghamton University is more than about having a great job - it is about having the opportunity to flourish in an exciting, engaging environment. Our faculty and staff appreciate Binghamton's collegial and inclusive culture and its commitment to excellence, education, innovation, and civic engagement. Our diverse campus community contributes to our success. Binghamton merges rigorous academics, distinguished faculty who value cutting-edge, and community-engaged research, teaching and service, exceptional staff, and ultramodern facilities to engage and challenge its 18,000+ students. Our high-achieving student body represents diverse experiences from first-generation college-goers to international students. Beyond their talent, these classmates share a desire to shape the future through technology, insight, intellectual exploration, and community service. Job Description: Department Overview: The Department of Social Work is entering an exciting period of growth and innovation. In addition to expanding its Bachelor of Social Work (BSW) and Master of Social Work (MSW) programs, the Department is actively developing a PhD program in Social Work, creating new opportunities for faculty leadership in doctoral education, research mentorship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and the advancement of the social work profession. The development of the PhD program reflects both the Department's growth and Binghamton University's broader commitment to expanding high-impact graduate and doctoral education within its R1 research environment. The Department is part of College of Community and Public Affairs (CCPA), an interdisciplinary college focused on preparing students for careers and leadership in public and community service. CCPA brings together Social Work, Human Development, Public Administration and Policy, Higher Education and Student Affairs, and Teaching, Learning and Educational Leadership, alongside the interdisciplinary Community Research and Action PhD program. The College emphasizes social justice, interdisciplinary collaboration, applied scholarship, and meaningful engagement with local, regional, national, and global communities. Located in downtown Binghamton, CCPA is closely connected to a broad network of community agencies, schools, health and human service organizations, government entities, and nonprofit partners. The Department offers a growing BSW program, a long-standing in-person MSW program, and an exclusively online MSW program, including full-time, part-time, and advanced standing pathways. The Department's programs are grounded in social justice, multisystem practice, community engagement, and a commitment to preparing social workers to address complex social problems across individuals, families, organizations, and communities. Job Description: Binghamton University is seeking faculty members at open rank Professor specializing in advanced quantitative methods or applied statistics to join its Department of Social Work within the CCPA. We seek a collegial and collaborative scholar whose expertise in advanced quantitative methods will strengthen the Department's research capacity and complement faculty whose primary expertise lies in substantive areas of social work, social policy, education, health, and human services. The candidate's substantive area of research specialization is open. We particularly welcome scholars who develop or apply advanced quantitative methods to complex, real-world data, including administrative, longitudinal, multilevel, multisystem, geospatial, linked, and community-engaged datasets. We are especially interested in scholarship that advances social justice, examines social and structural determinants of health and well-being, informs equitable policy and practice, and addresses systemic inequities affecting marginalized and historically underserved populations. Key Responsibilities: Serve as a methodological partner to faculty and graduate students, and interdisciplinary research teams across the full research lifecycle, including: study design measurement data management and linkage statistical analysis interpretation grant development dissemination Contribute intellectually to substantive research initiatives and promote rigorous, transparent, and responsible use of quantitative methods. Maintain an active program of independent and/or collaborative scholarship appropriate for tenure and promotion, with opportunities to pursue external funding as a principal investigator, co-investigator, methodological collaborator, or statistical lead. Teach and develop advanced level statistics and quantitative methods courses that are rigorous, accessible, and applicable to social work and related social and behavioral sciences. Teaching may include areas such as: multilevel and longitudinal modeling latent-variable methods causal inference psychometrics and measurement Bayesian methods missing-data methods computational methods machine learning reproducible research practices Binghamton University offers a collegial, collaborative environment within a nationally recognized R1 research institution. Faculty benefit from competitive salaries, research support and start-up resources, and extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and community-engaged scholarship. Binghamton's research enterprise is supported by substantial institutional investment and a university-wide commitment to translating research into meaningful impact. Diversity and Inclusion: Binghamton University remains committed to and supports diversity and inclusion as integral to academic excellence. We actively seek applications from underrepresented racial/ethnic groups, sexual minorities, veterans, persons with disabilities, and women. Requirements: Requirements: Doctoral degree conferred by August 1, 2027 in statistics, biostatistics, quantitative psychology, or a closely aligned discipline Candidates whose doctorates are in social work, psychology, public health, education, economics, sociology, data science, public policy, or another closely related field will also be considered if they demonstrate a strong background in advanced quantitative analysis Experience working with researchers across multiple substantive areas and adapting quantitative methods The ability to translate substantive research questions into rigorous and feasible quantitative designs and analytic plans Demonstrated high-quality teaching, advising, and student mentorship A record of independent and collaborative scholarship Preferred: Expertise analyzing complex datasets Experience working with large-scale data from human-service systems or other real-world settings Experience contributing to externally funded, team-based research Demonstrated ability to communicate quantitative methods to students and colleagues with varied levels of statistical preparation Experience with reproducible and responsible research practices, (e.g., transparent analytic workflows, data documentation, and appropriate data-sharing practices) Scholarship addressing social determinants of health and well-being, systemic or structural inequities Experience incorporating critical, anti-racist, or equity-centered perspectives into quantitative research design, measurement, analysis, and interpretation Potential to contribute to interdisciplinary research initiatives Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. If you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet eligibility requirements. Additionally, please note that Binghamton University is not an E-Verify employer. Additional Information: Binghamton University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its services, programs, and activities. To request reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, please contact the ADA Coordinator by completing the following Request Form . The State University of New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. It is the policy of Binghamton University to provide for and promote equal opportunity employment, compensation, and other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the basis of age, race, color, religion, disability, national origin, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, veteran or military service member status, marital status, domestic violence victim status, genetic predisposition or carrier status, or arrest and/or criminal conviction record unless based upon a bona fide occupational qualification or other exception. As required by title IX and its implementing regulations, Binghamton University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the educational programs and activities which it operates. This requirement extends to employment and admission. Inquiries about sex discrimination may be directed to the University Title IX Coordinator or directly to the Office of Civil Rights (OCR). Contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and OCR, as well as the University's complete Non-Discrimination Notice may be found here. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation. If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor's Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Binghamton University is a tobacco-free campus effective August 1, 2017. Offers of employment will be contingent upon successful completion of a pre-employment background check and verification of degree(s) and credentials. Application Instructions: All applicants must apply via Interview Exchange: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205023 Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until 1/15/2027. Please submit: 1. Resume, 2. Cover letter, 3. Portfolio of collaborative contributions containing two or three brief examples of collaborative projects. For each example, applicants should identify: The substantive research question and disciplinary context; Their role in the collaboration; The methodological challenges involved; Their intellectual and technical contributions; How they communicated with substantive collaborators; and The resulting products or outcomes. Examples may include collaborative publications, funded or submitted grant proposals, statistical analysis plans, program evaluations, data-linkage projects, reproducible analytic                             workflows, policy reports, methodological appendices, or other relevant products. Applicants should clarify their individual contributions to co-authored work. 4. Contact information for three professional references You may add additional files/documents after uploading your resume. After you fill out your contact information, you will be directed to the upload page. Please login to check/edit your profile or to upload additional documents: http://binghamton.interviewexchange.com/login.jsp. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Binghamton University State University of New York 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY 13902"
